{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase-1 runtime RCA from Gerrit 1806 PS1 run 31869753711: the macOS default suite itself passed 13,397/65-skipped in 1,123.77s, then the release step spent about 47.3s recursively measuring 1,128,280 KiB / 231,474 files and failed during rm -rf. The failing paths are tests/unit/workflow snapshot fixtures whose production contract deliberately chmods published snapshot directories/files read-only (src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py); hosted macOS emitted Permission denied for those exact .rebar/run_snapshots paths, left DEFAULT_BASETEMP nonempty, and skipped integration. The always-run final reporter still detached exact /dev/disk8 successfully, so no device leaked. Confirmed mechanism: the new success-path release assumes pytest basetemp is recursively removable, but the suite intentionally leaves owner-nonwritable snapshot trees; restore owner write permission recursively on the already exact-path-validated DEFAULT_BASETEMP before removal. This is a cleanup-contract defect, not a test assertion failure. Separately, this first treatment sample was slower than control (18m43s default versus 17m26s exact control), so RAM-disk efficacy remains unproven pending the already-running duplicate/organic samples; do not treat the cleanup repair as performance proof."},"env_id":"0993f6a8-7a6a-4707-a671-c5bbb2691444","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786777221167972001,"uuid":"ce87bba5-7c6d-4eff-b297-48796b4baf5b"}